by nicolas-grekas at 2026-09-04T14:32:16Z

Follow-up noted during the review, out of scope for this PR: reclaiming application option names for commands.

Today a command cannot declare an option named `verbose`, `quiet`, `ansi`, `no-interaction`, `help` or `version` (nor `env`, `no-debug` or `profile` in a Symfony application), and none of their shortcuts, because the application definition is merged into every command definition and a clash throws. Per-level parsing opens an additive route: a command, or a tree level, may declare a shadowing option; at that level the command's definition wins, and the application option stays reachable before the command name or at an ancestor level, so `docker compose down -v` can mean "remove volumes" while `docker -v compose down` keeps the global verbosity. It needs the merge and the level definitions to skip shadowed application options, `configureIO()` to re-derive verbosity, ansi and interactivity from the bound inputs once the command is known, and the option replay of this PR to skip shadowed names. Nobody has such a command today since the clash throws, so it can come as its own PR on top of this one.

A rule "application options only before the command name" for 9.0 was considered for the same goal and dropped: trailing `-vvv`, `-n` and `-e prod` sit in every script and CI job.

