by nicolas-grekas at 2026-09-08T06:46:39Z

@stof it runs, and only when it is the command being invoked. Nothing changes for a node that defines `execute()` (or `__invoke()`, or `setCode()`): the tree only decides which command the input resolves to, and that command runs alone.

With `docker`, `docker:compose` and `docker:compose:up` all defining `execute()`:

```
docker compose up   ->  docker:compose:up::execute
docker compose      ->  docker:compose::execute
docker              ->  docker::execute
docker:compose      ->  docker:compose::execute   (unchanged colon form)
```

So an intermediate `execute()` is what a node runs when the invocation stops there, exactly like today when that command is called by its colon name. Ancestor levels are parsed, never executed, and their bound input stays readable from the leaf through `CommandChain`.

The listing behavior only fills the gap: a node **without** code of its own would have thrown "You must override the execute() method"; it now lists its sub-commands on the error output with exit code 1, like a bare namespace. Defining `execute()` opts out of that listing, since the command has something to do.

