by nicolas-grekas at 2026-09-08T06:46:43Z

@Neirda24 it works, and the two are independent, because each level parses against its own definition:

```
docker --context=a compose up --context=b   ->  up sees "b",             docker level holds "a"
docker --context=a compose up               ->  up sees its own default, docker level holds "a"
docker compose up --context=b               ->  up sees "b",             docker level holds its default
docker -c a compose up -c b                 ->  same, shortcuts included
```

No override and no clash: `--context` before `compose` belongs to `docker`, `--context` after `up` belongs to `up`. The leaf reads its own value from `$input` as usual, and the ancestor value through the chain:

```php
$chain->getInput('docker')->getOption('context');
```

If only `docker` declares the option, then `docker --context=a compose up` binds it at the `docker` level, while `docker compose up --context=a` fails with `The "--context" option does not exist.`, since `up` does not declare it: options are per level, and a child does not inherit its parent's ones. The exception is the application's own options (`-v`, `-n`, `--env`, …), which every level accepts and which are carried over to the leaf input wherever they appear.

What is not possible today is a command declaring an option that collides with an **application** option, `--verbose` or `-v` for instance, because the application definition is merged into every command. Making a level shadow those is the follow-up I described [above](https://github.com/symfony/symfony/pull/65825#issuecomment-5541950457).

